    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 012306\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n306 PM PST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Numerous showers and thunderstorms will continue through late \n  afternoon before tapering off this evening. Brief heavy rain and\n  lightning will be possible. \n\n* Conditions dry out on Monday with northerly breezes.\n  Temperatures will start slightly below normal early in the week\n  and climb slightly above normal by next weekend. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through next weekend.\n\nBroad shield of subtropical moisture and steady moderate to\nlocally heavy rainfall has shifted eastward this afternoon to\nbecome focused more across eastern Arizona. Overnight rainfall\ntotals across our region range from a tenth-of an inch or less\nacross the Western Mojave Desert and the valleys of Inyo County,\nto roughly 0.25-0.75 across the eastern Mojave Desert including\nLas Vegas. The heaviest rainfall from the event has focused across\neastern Lincoln and Mohave Counties, where widespread rainfall\ntotals of 1.5-4 inches have been observed. Heavy rain across the\nBig Sandy watershed have led to near record flows in Burro Creek\nand the Big Sandy which are ongoing this afternoon. \n\nModest instability developing ahead of the trough axis is\ncurrently fostering thunderstorm development across the Death\nValley and Amargosa Valley regions that will continue to develop\ntowards the Spring Mountains through the afternoon with localized\nheavy downpours. A few storms will also develop across northwest\nArizona along a developing precipitation axis. All this activity\nwill gradually wane through the evening with the loss of daytime \nheating and the trough axis shifting east. \n\nThe remainder of the week ahead looks fairly benign with dry\nconditions. Temperatures will start out slightly below normal but\nclimb slightly above normal by next weekend as heights rise across\nthe region. Lookout for some patchy fog in the cooler valleys the\nnext few mornings with ideal radiational cooling conditions taking\nplace and elevated moisture levels persisting across the region. \n\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package... \nScattered showers and isolated storms linger around 01z, with drier \nweather and improving sky conditions this evening. Winds this \nevening and overnight will most likely be from a westerly or \nsouthwesterly direction, but there remains a ~30% chance that \nnorthwest winds take hold. Higher confidence in northwest winds \narriving mid-morning on Monday, but should weaken and turn \nnortheasterly in the afternoon. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Scattered showers and \nisolated storms continue to push east this evening, exiting the \nforecast area by at least midnight. Gusty, erratic winds are \npossible near the strongest convection, along with lower CIGs and \nreduced visiblity within precipitation. Behind this activity, sky \nconditions are expected to rapidly improve. Gusty southwest to west \nwinds continue across the Mojave Desert this afternoon, with gusty \nnorthwest winds in the southern Great Basin. Winds forecast to \nsubside overnight, favoring a northerly direction on Monday. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Outler\nAVIATION...Woods\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
